    "result_text": "The build is green. Let me confirm the membership number correctly reflects the fiscal year (the audit log showed `NSW-16E6609E-M27` for a `2026-08-10` registration date \u2014 FY27, correct since August is after 1 July).\n\nAll changes are complete and the test suite passes. Here's a summary of what I implemented for the fiscal-year change:\n\n**Fiscal year definition** \u2014 The fiscal year starts on 1 July, so a date on/after 1 July belongs to the fiscal year ending in the *following* calendar year (e.g. `2026-08-10` \u2192 FY27, `2026-05-10` \u2192 FY26). I encoded this as a single shared helper `Owner.fiscalYear(LocalDate)` returning the ending calendar year, so all three derived values use one source of truth.\n\n**Changes:**\n\n1. **`Owner.java`**\n   - Added `static int fiscalYear(LocalDate)` helper.\n   - Added `getFiscalYear()` \u2192 `\"FY<YY>\"` (two digits of the fiscal year), derived from the stored `registrationDate` (which the controller already stores business-day-adjusted); `null` when no registration date.\n   - Changed tenure in `getMembershipPoints()` from `DAYS.between(...) > 365` to *elapsed fiscal years* `fiscalYear(now) - fiscalYear(registrationDate) > 1`.\n\n2. **`OwnerRestControllerV1.java`** \u2014 `generateMembershipNumber` now uses `Owner.fiscalYear(registrationDate) % 100` for the `-M<YY>` segment instead of the calendar-year digits.\n\n3. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 Added the read-only `fiscalYear` property to the Owner schema (so the generated `OwnerDto` gets the field and MapStruct auto-maps it by name), and updated the `membershipNumber` and `membershipPoints` descriptions to reflect the fiscal-year basis.\n\nThe new acceptance test `coreReturnsFiscalYear` passes (asserts `fiscalYear` matches `FY\\d{2}`), and the full suite builds green. The audit log confirms end-to-end behaviour: an owner registered `2026-08-10` gets membership number `\u2026-M27` (FY27).\n\nOne judgment call worth flagging: the spec said \"tenure must be counted in elapsed fiscal years rather than 365-day windows\" without pinning the exact threshold. I mapped the old \">365 days\" (more than one year) to \">1 elapsed fiscal year\". If you'd prefer `>= 1` (i.e. crossing a single 1-July boundary earns the points), that's a one-line change.",
